import{_ as t,o,c as n,a3 as a}from"./chunks/framework.DAK02U1-.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"PGO","description":"","frontmatter":{"head":[["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"PGO | clice.io"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://docs.clice.io/xclang/design/pgo"}],["meta",{"property":"og:image","content":"https://docs.clice.io/og.png"}],["meta",{"property":"og:image:width","content":"1200"}],["meta",{"property":"og:image:height","content":"630"}],["meta",{"property":"og:locale","content":"en_US"}],["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://docs.clice.io/xclang/design/pgo"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://docs.clice.io/xclang/design/pgo"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://docs.clice.io/xclang/design/pgo"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"xclang/design/pgo.md","filePath":"en/xclang/design/pgo.md","lastUpdated":1791329591000}'),i={name:"xclang/design/pgo.md"};function r(s,e,d,l,h,c){return o(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="pgo" tabindex="-1">PGO <a class="header-anchor" href="#pgo" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;PGO&quot;">​</a></h1><p>xclang&#39;s clang and lld are built with profile-guided optimization (PGO) and ThinLTO on every host, from one profile recorded on Linux. This page is the home of xclang&#39;s speed measurements; each names its release.</p><h2 id="summary" tabindex="-1">Summary <a class="header-anchor" href="#summary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Summary&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The profile comes from compiling real code the way builds and editors do. Frontend instrumentation makes one profile, recorded on Linux, fit all six hosts. On C++, 23.1.2.6 compiles as fast as LLVM&#39;s own release builds on Linux and macOS, and takes 11 to 22% less time than LLVM&#39;s build on Windows x64. In 23.1.2.1, the same build without the profile took 1.15 to 1.37 times as long as LLVM&#39;s.</p><h2 id="the-training" tabindex="-1">The Training <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-training" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Training&quot;">​</a></h2><p>PGO optimizes a program for the work it was profiled on, so the training set decides what gets faster. xclang&#39;s instrumented clang, clang-scan-deps and lld do the work the toolchain is used for, by builds and by editors (<a href="https://github.com/clice-io/xclang/blob/main/toolchain/pgo/train.ts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">toolchain/pgo/train.ts</a>):</p><ul><li>C and C++ sources (sqlite, abseil) at <code>-O0 -g</code> and <code>-O2</code>, for x86_64 and aarch64 Linux.</li><li>Precompiled headers: one shared header, and the preamble of every abseil source. An editor precompiles a preamble once per file, then parses the rest of the file and runs code completion on it.</li><li>C++20 modules: libc++&#39;s <code>std</code> and <code>std.compat</code>, real ones (magic_enum, and Vulkan-Hpp&#39;s, the largest in common use), a wrapped header-only library (nlohmann/json), a module of partitions, and their importers. They build in two phases at <code>-O2</code>, and in one phase with reduced module files at <code>-O0 -g</code>. The P1689 dependency scanning runs as CMake runs it.</li><li>Code completion requests.</li><li>ELF, ThinLTO and COFF (MinGW) links through lld.</li></ul><p>About 1700 compiler runs in 23 minutes give one profile per release. It is published with the release (<code>xclang-&lt;version&gt;.profdata</code>).</p><p><strong>Not in the training</strong>: Objective-C, clang-cl and MSVC-mode parsing, Mach-O links and ld64.lld, aarch64 COFF links, and code paths that only run on a Windows or macOS host. The sanitizers are not in it either, nor the libraries in libclang that clang itself does not use (clang-tidy&#39;s, clangIndex, IncludeCleaner). That code gets the layout and inlining the profile gives it from the rest, not tuned for itself.</p><h2 id="one-profile-for-every-host" tabindex="-1">One Profile for Every Host <a class="header-anchor" href="#one-profile-for-every-host" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One Profile for Every Host&quot;">​</a></h2><p>clang has two kinds of instrumentation. The choice decides whether a profile recorded on one host fits another.</p><ul><li><strong>IR instrumentation</strong> (<code>-fprofile-generate</code>) counts on LLVM IR after early optimization passes. It identifies a function by a hash of its control flow graph at that point. Those passes use the cost models of the target, so the same function has a different control flow on x86_64 and arm64. A profile recorded on one does not match the other.</li><li><strong>Frontend instrumentation</strong> (<code>-fprofile-instr-generate</code>, LLVM&#39;s <code>LLVM_BUILD_INSTRUMENTED=Frontend</code>) counts on the clang AST, and hashes a function from its source. The same source gives the same hash on every target.</li></ul><p>xclang uses frontend instrumentation. In experiments before 23.1.2.1, a profile recorded on Linux x64 matched all but 0 or 1 of 113,000 functions on each of the six hosts. The speed it gave was within noise of per-host IR profiles merged from all six. The table gives the compile time of abseil / LLVM&#39;s <code>Sema</code>, relative to no PGO, as the median of five runners:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>host</th><th>frontend, Linux profile</th><th>IR, six profiles merged</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Linux x64</td><td>0.725 / 0.727</td><td>0.722 / 0.721</td></tr><tr><td>Linux arm64</td><td>0.814 / 0.800</td><td>0.826 / 0.844</td></tr><tr><td>macOS arm64</td><td>0.905 / 0.818</td><td>0.895 / 0.864</td></tr><tr><td>Windows x64</td><td>0.771 / 0.792</td><td>0.764 / 0.820</td></tr></tbody></table><p>So the training runs once, on one Linux runner, and its profile builds every host&#39;s toolchain. There is no instrumented build or training per host, no emulation, and the same profile everywhere.</p><h3 id="the-remapping-file" tabindex="-1">The Remapping File <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-remapping-file" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Remapping File&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A function&#39;s profile is found by its mangled name, and the same source type mangles differently across ABIs. <code>size_t</code> and <code>uint64_t</code> are <code>unsigned long</code> (<code>m</code>) on Linux but <code>unsigned long long</code> (<code>y</code>) on Windows, and <code>uint64_t</code> is <code>unsigned long long</code> on macOS too. <code>int64_t</code> differs the same way, <code>l</code> against <code>x</code>. A function taking a <code>size_t</code> thus has another name on Windows than in the Linux profile, and gets no counts.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/clice-io/xclang/blob/main/toolchain/pgo/remap.txt" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">toolchain/pgo/remap.txt</a> tells clang that these manglings are the same (<code>-fprofile-remapping-file</code>). In the same experiments, it took macOS arm64 from 0.896 / 0.923 to 0.794 / 0.845, and Windows x64 from 0.787 / 0.824 to 0.772 / 0.829.</p><p>Some functions the remapping cannot match:</p><ul><li>On Windows, 230 functions whose template arguments are integer literals of those types, 1.8% of the counts.</li><li>Functions with internal linkage: 122 on Windows and 39 on macOS, 0.06% and 0.02% of the counts. Frontend profile names of local functions are <code>&lt;file&gt;:&lt;mangled&gt;</code>, and LLVM&#39;s remapper does not split the file name off.</li></ul><p>Neither is reported upstream.</p><h2 id="what-it-buys" tabindex="-1">What It Buys <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-it-buys" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What It Buys&quot;">​</a></h2><p>bench.yml times compiles on machines of each host, for that host&#39;s target (tests/bench/bench.ts). The code is code the training never saw:</p><ul><li>ten of fmt&#39;s tests: heavy templates, C++20;</li><li>the C sources of lua;</li><li>precompiling libc++&#39;s <code>std</code> module.</li></ul><p>Each is compiled at <code>-fsyntax-only</code>, <code>-O0 -g</code> and <code>-O2</code>. Every file is compiled alone, one after another. Rounds interleave the compilers in a rotating order on the same machine, and the median of the rounds is taken. The report is the median, over several machines, of each machine&#39;s ratio to the reference. Runner noise is large: read a difference of a few percent as none.</p><p>The reference is LLVM&#39;s own release build of the same version. LLVM builds it with PGO and ThinLTO on Linux and macOS, BOLT-optimized on Linux x64, and with PGO but no LTO on Windows. LLVM has no build for x86_64 macOS; there, the reference is xclang itself.</p><h3 id="_23-1-2-6" tabindex="-1">23.1.2.6 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_23-1-2-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;23.1.2.6&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Time relative to LLVM 23.1.2&#39;s release build, median of five runners per host, 2026-10-06 (<a href="https://github.com/clice-io/xclang/actions/runs/37356476645" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">run 37356476645</a>, 23.1.2.6&#39;s own archives, nothing rebuilt). Linux x64 has four runners, because one job failed listing sizes, before measuring. The <code>llvm.exe</code> rows run xclang&#39;s Windows clang directly instead of through the <code>clang++.exe</code> launcher.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>host</th><th>fmt (syntax / O0 / O2)</th><th>lua (syntax / O0 / O2)</th><th>std (O0 / O2)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Linux x64</td><td>1.035 / 1.009 / 1.069</td><td>1.118 / 1.062 / 1.109</td><td>1.013 / 1.022</td></tr><tr><td>Linux arm64</td><td>1.000 / 0.976 / 1.042</td><td>1.082 / 1.011 / 1.075</td><td>0.970 / 0.994</td></tr><tr><td>macOS arm64</td><td>0.902 / 0.935 / 1.014</td><td>1.169 / 0.971 / 0.959</td><td>0.980 / 0.890</td></tr><tr><td>Windows x64</td><td>0.798 / 0.825 / 0.890</td><td>1.083 / 1.050 / 1.001</td><td>0.777 / 0.849</td></tr><tr><td>Windows x64, <code>llvm.exe</code></td><td>0.798 / 0.809 / 0.885</td><td>0.968 / 0.960 / 0.974</td><td>0.821 / 0.844</td></tr><tr><td>Windows arm64</td><td>0.888 / 0.906 / 0.954</td><td>1.271 / 1.212 / 1.167</td><td>0.925 / 0.940</td></tr><tr><td>Windows arm64, <code>llvm.exe</code></td><td>0.860 / 0.898 / 0.947</td><td>1.019 / 1.065 / 1.056</td><td>0.915 / 0.932</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Apple&#39;s clang ran on the same runners. On arm64, relative to LLVM&#39;s build, it took fmt 1.097 / 1.207 / 1.206 and lua 0.879 / 0.875 / 1.004. On x86_64, where LLVM has no build, relative to xclang, it took fmt 1.168 / 1.223 / 1.221 and lua 0.841 / 0.973 / 0.990.</p><p>What the numbers say, read loosely:</p><ul><li><strong>Linux and macOS</strong>: on C++, xclang is as fast as LLVM&#39;s own PGO and ThinLTO builds. On lua&#39;s C it takes up to 17% more time. On Linux x64, whose LLVM clang is also BOLT-optimized, xclang takes up to 7% more time on C++ and 12% on C.</li><li><strong>Windows</strong>: against LLVM&#39;s MSVC-built clang (PGO, no LTO), xclang&#39;s MinGW-built clang with PGO and ThinLTO takes 11 to 22% less time on C++ on x64, and 5 to 11% less on arm64.</li><li><strong>The Windows launcher costs a process.</strong> lua&#39;s sources are small C files, each compiled in a few dozen milliseconds. Starting <code>clang++.exe</code>, which then starts <code>llvm.exe</code>, is a visible part of that time: up to 12% more on x64 and 25% more on arm64 than running <code>llvm.exe clang++</code> directly. On C++ with real work per file, it does not show (<a href="./windows#the-launchers">the launchers</a>).</li><li><strong>Apple&#39;s clang</strong> takes 17 to 29% more time than xclang on fmt, on both macOS architectures. On lua&#39;s C, it takes between 25% less and 5% more.</li></ul><h3 id="_23-1-2-1" tabindex="-1">23.1.2.1 <a class="header-anchor" href="#_23-1-2-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;23.1.2.1&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Time relative to LLVM 23.1.2&#39;s release build, median of three runners (<a href="https://github.com/clice-io/xclang/actions/runs/36249036595" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">run 36249036595</a> for Linux and macOS, <a href="https://github.com/clice-io/xclang/actions/runs/36250216010" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">run 36250216010</a> for Windows). &quot;no PGO&quot; is the same build without the profile; <code>llvm.exe</code> runs xclang&#39;s Windows clang without the launcher.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>host</th><th>fmt (syntax / O0 / O2)</th><th>lua</th><th>std (O0 / O2)</th><th>no PGO, fmt</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Linux x64</td><td>1.031 / 1.006 / 1.046</td><td>1.106 / 1.061 / 1.098</td><td>1.047 / 1.022</td><td>1.279 / 1.250 / 1.244</td></tr><tr><td>Linux arm64</td><td>0.972 / 0.957 / 1.018</td><td>1.012 / 0.968 / 1.050</td><td>0.959 / 0.964</td><td>1.283 / 1.237 / 1.272</td></tr><tr><td>macOS arm64</td><td>0.986 / 0.983 / 1.050</td><td>0.948 / 0.804 / 0.994</td><td>0.860 / 0.955</td><td>1.367 / 1.323 / 1.353</td></tr><tr><td>Windows x64</td><td>0.809 / 0.830 / 0.893</td><td>1.075 / 1.049 / 1.041 (<code>llvm.exe</code>: 1.044 / 0.987 / 1.020)</td><td>0.885 / 1.021</td><td>1.226 / 1.155 / 1.227</td></tr><tr><td>Windows arm64</td><td>0.899 / 0.916 / 0.958</td><td>1.332 / 1.238 / 1.187 (<code>llvm.exe</code>: 1.090 / 1.058 / 1.079)</td><td>0.944 / 0.939</td><td>1.178 / 1.150 / 1.162</td></tr></tbody></table><p>What this run adds, with the same build without its profile:</p><ul><li><p><strong>The profile takes 16 to 34% off the time</strong> of fmt&#39;s compiles. Without it, the same build takes 1.15 to 1.37 times as long as LLVM&#39;s; with it, about as long.</p></li><li><p><strong>x86_64 macOS was the exception.</strong> There, xclang without its profile was not slower than with it (0.84 to 1.12, relative to xclang), and Apple&#39;s clang was faster than xclang (0.89 to 1.08 on fmt, 0.57 to 0.87 on lua).</p><p>23.1.2.6&#39;s run, which has no build without the profile, has Apple&#39;s clang 17 to 22% slower on fmt. The x86_64 macOS toolchain is cross-compiled on arm64 macOS with the same profile, and the <code>macos-15-intel</code> runners are the noisiest of the six. Whether the profile helps there is not known.</p></li></ul><h2 id="not-yet-supported" tabindex="-1">Not Yet Supported <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-yet-supported" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not Yet Supported&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="./roadmap#bolt">BOLT</a>, which LLVM&#39;s Linux x64 release uses on top of PGO</td><td><span class="status-badge note">In research</span></td></tr><tr><td><a href="./roadmap#pgo-training">A training on more of the toolchain&#39;s work</a>: Objective-C, clang-cl, Mach-O links, clang-tidy&#39;s checks</td><td><span class="status-badge note">Planned</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The training is widened between releases, not while one is pending.</p>',40)])])}const u=t(i,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
